"File input parameters to tools - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/file-input-parameters-to-tools/1371839)\nciteturn30969view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn30968view2\",\"lineno\":25}); Total lines: 224\nL0: cite0†OpenAI Developer Community L1: # cite1†File input parameters to tools L2: \nL3: cite2†API L4: \nL5: cite3†Jaime_Cham January 16, 2026, 7:59pm 1\nL6: In cite4†Build your MCP server†developers.openai.com it is specified that tool input properties that receive file inputs should be specified as:\nL7: ```\nL8: \nL9:     `    imageToProcess: {\nL10:           type: \"object\",\nL11:           properties: {\nL12:             download_url: { type: \"string\" },\nL13:             file_id: { type: \"string\" },\nL14:           },\nL15:           required: [\"download_url\", \"file_id\"],\nL16:           additionalProperties: false,\nL17:         },\nL18:     `\nL19: However, when I do this, ChatGPT refuses to call that tool (it fails silently). When I asked ChatGPT why it cannot call that tool it says that I defined it as taking a `string`. Further questioning has it telling me this is by design (see attached transcript below).\nL20: \nL21: So this is something permanent we have to work around to? should this be documented somewhere if so?\nL22: \nL23: Thanks!\nL24: \nL25: * * *\nL26: \nL27: * * *\nL28: \nL29: * * *\nL30: \nL31: * * *\nL32: \nL33: * * *\nL34: \nL35: * * *\nL36: Short answer: this discrepancy is expected behavior, and it’s coming from the Actions tool normalization layer, not from you sending the definition “wrong”.\nL37: \nL38: Long answer below, step-by-step, grounded in how OpenAI Actions actually work today.\nL39: \nL40: * * *\nL41: ## TL;DR\nL42: \nL43: You are defining the tool correctly (object with `{ download_url, file_id }`), but:\nL44: \nL45: > The Actions runtime rewrites file parameters into `string | null` (file IDs) when exposing the callable tool interface to the model.\nL46: \nL47: What you see when I list tools is the normalized invocation surface, not your raw JSON Schema.\nL48: \nL49: * * *\nL50: \nL51: ## What’s actually happening\nL52: ### cite5†Image: :one:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com There are three representations of “a file”\nL53: \nL54: In the Actions system, files exist at three different layers:\nL55: \nL56: Layer  | Representation\nL57: --- | ---\nL58: UI / Widget schema  | `{ file_id, download_url }` object\nL59: Tool definition (authoring)  | JSON Schema you wrote\nL60: LLM invocation surface  | `string` (file_id only)\nL61: \nL62: You’re looking at layer 3 when I report:\nL63: \nL64:     `file_0: string | null\nL65:     `\nL66: \nL67: That is intentional canonicalization.\nL68: \nL69: * * *\nL70: ### cite6†Image: :two:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com `openai/fileParams` triggers coercion\nL71: \nL72: This line is the key:\nL73: \nL74:     `\"openai/fileParams\": [\"file_0\", \"file_1\", \"file_2\"]\nL75:     `\nL76: \nL77: When the Actions platform sees this, it:\nL78: \nL79:   1. Marks those params as file slots\nL80:   2. Ensures the model only ever passes file IDs\nL81:   3. Handles download URLs internally (or ignores them)\nL82: \nL83: So even if your schema says:\nL84: \nL85:     `file_0: {\nL86:       file_id: string,\nL87:       download_url: string\nL88:     }\nL89:     `\nL90: …the exposed callable tool becomes:\nL91: \nL92:     `file_0: string | null\nL93:     `\nL94: \nL95: This is by design.\nL96: \nL97: * * *\nL98: ### cite7†Image: :three:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com Why the UI still needs `{ download_url, file_id }`\nL99: \nL100: The VideoUploader widget and templates need richer metadata to:\nL101: \nL102:   * render previews\nL103:   * show filenames / sizes\nL104:   * manage upload lifecycle\nL105: \nL106: That’s why your template schema still shows objects:\nL107: \nL108:     `\"paramsSchema\": {\nL109:       \"file_0\": {\nL110:         \"type\": \"object\",\nL111:         \"properties\": {\nL112:           \"file_id\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\nL113:           \"download_url\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\nL114:         }\nL115:       }\nL116:     }\nL117:     `\nL118: This schema is UI-facing, not LLM-facing.\nL119: \nL120: * * *\nL121: ### cite8†Image: :four:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com Why I see `string` when listing tools\nL122: \nL123: When I call:\nL124: \nL125:     `api_tool.list_resources(only_tools=true)\nL126:     `\nL127: \nL128: I am shown the model-facing invocation contract, which is:\nL129: \nL130:     `file_0: string | null\nL131:     `\nL132: \nL133: That’s the post-normalization version after:\nL134: \nL135:   * `openai/fileParams` handling\nL136:   * widget binding\nL137:   * internal file registry wiring\nL138: \nL139: I am not seeing your raw schema anymore.\nL140: \nL141: * * *\nL142: ## This is not a bug (but it is confusing)\nL143: \nL144: This behavior is:\nL145: \nL146:   * undocumented publicly\nL147:   * non-obvious\nL148:   * extremely easy to misinterpret\nL149: \nL150: But it is consistent with:\nL151: \nL152:   * first-party OpenAI Actions\nL153:   * file-aware tools (image, audio, video)\nL154:   * widget-backed file upload flows\nL155: \nL156: You did nothing wrong.\nL157: \nL158: * * *\nL159: \nL160: ## Important implication for your design\nL161: ### cite9†Image: :cross_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com You cannot force the model to pass `{ file_id, download_url }`\nL162: \nL163: If a param is listed under `openai/fileParams`, the model will always pass a string file ID.\nL164: \nL165: So this earlier idea:\nL166: \nL167: > “Change `edit_video` to accept file_0 objects”\nL168: \nL169: cite9†Image: :cross_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com will never work at invocation time, even if the schema allows it.\nL170: \nL171: * * *\nL172: ## cite10†Image: :white_check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com The correct mental model\nL173: \nL174: Concern  | What to rely on\nL175: --- | ---\nL176: UI / widgets  | `{ file_id, download_url }`\nL177: Tool invocation  | `string` (file_id)\nL178: Backend  | Fetch file by ID\nL179: \nL180: Your backend should treat `file_0` as:\nL181: \nL182:     `const fileId = params.file_0\nL183:     `\nL184: \nL185: and never expect `download_url` from the model.\nL186: \nL187: * * *\nL188: ## Why this exists (historical reason)\nL189: \nL190: Internally, OpenAI:\nL191: \nL192:   * stores files in a secure registry\nL193:   * does not want models fabricating URLs\nL194:   * guarantees file access via ID only\nL195: \nL196: So file params are lowered to IDs at the model boundary.\nL197: \nL198: * * *\nL199: ## Final verdict\nL200: \nL201: cite11†Image: :check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com You are sending the tool definition correctly\nL202: cite11†Image: :check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com The UI schema is correct\nL203: cite11†Image: :check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com The widget schema is correct\nL204: cite11†Image: :check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com The platform is intentionally rewriting file params\nL205: cite11†Image: :check_mark:†emoji.discourse-cdn.com The reported `string | null` type is expected\nL206: \nL207: If you want, next I can:\nL208:   * diagram the full file lifecycle\nL209:   * show the exact backend handler pattern\nL210:   * or help you clean up docs so future devs don’t fall into this trap\nL211: \nL212: cite3†Jaime_Cham January 17, 2026, 2:39am 2\nL213: \nL214: Further follow up, where I made it accept either the string or the object, has me seeing that ChatGPT doesn’t send either! Instead of a file_id, it tries to send a local path, like `/mnt/data/best run niseko (CUT).mp4`, which is a path to a file in the built-in container, and completely unaccesible.\nL215: \nL216: Help!\nL217:   * cite0†Home L218:   * cite12†Categories L219:   * cite13†Guidelines L220:   * cite14†Terms of Service†openai.com L221:   * cite15†Privacy Policy†openai.com L222: \nL223: Powered by cite16†Discourse†www.discourse.org , best viewed with JavaScript enabled\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn30969view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn30968view0\",\"id\":7}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://community.openai.com/u/Sean-Der: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRealtime API unreliable over SIP - #13 by Sean-Der - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/realtime-api-unreliable-over-sip/1366350/13)\nciteturn30969view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn30968view0\",\"id\":8}); Total lines: 30\nL0: cite0†OpenAI Developer Community L1: # cite1†Realtime API unreliable over SIP L2: \nL3: cite2†API L4: \nL5: cite3†realtime , cite4†sip L6: \nL7: cite5†Sean-Der January 17, 2026, 3:28am 13\nL8: \nL9: I have deployed all our planned fixes/improvements! I am seeing webhooks at ~1 second now. If people still see issues with Webhooks please reach out. These are the changes I made.\nL10:   * SIP Servers are now deployed to four regions (instead of just one)\nL11:   * Webhooks would get dropped because a service internally was restarting from OOM\nL12:   * +1second of latency was introduced by internal service communication, this was removed.\nL13:   * You will no longer be notified about old calls. Before you could get webhooks from a call that occured before 30 seconds ago.\nL14: \nL15: cite6†SIP calls fail for a few minutes every day L16: cite7†Consistently 3–5s (sometimes 7s) INVITE→realtime.call.incoming delay on SIP Realtime; accept is <1s. Any guidance? L17: \nL18: cite8†Twilio - OpenAI SIP Trunk calls failing with '400 Bad Request' error L19: \nL20: cite9†Troubleshooting SIP and Asterisk communication issues L21: \nL22: cite10†Twilio - OpenAI SIP Trunk calls failing with '400 Bad Request' error L23: \nL24:   * cite0†Home L25:   * cite11†Categories L26:   * cite12†Guidelines L27:   * cite13†Terms of Service†openai.com L28:   * cite14†Privacy Policy†openai.com L29: Powered by cite15†Discourse†www.discourse.org , best viewed with JavaScript enabled\n"
